\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761201 1072 #uuid "66ea80ee-e83a-580d-84c5-654898396cd8" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761201 1074 "![Completed vector route](images/70/7052d3c533e32a07ba1e612d92b7b81b74e6aaf34a12f6ca53a7ce0f313ef787.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $1$ square left and $1$ square down. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square left and $1$ square down. Thus $OX=1|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $6$ squares right and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares left and $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=\\mathbf{a}-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=1$ and $\\mu=-\\frac{3}{2}$." 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761202 1080 "1" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761202 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761202 1083 #uuid "678af90a-d723-58c8-81b1-a65d6bf49d2e" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761199 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761211 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761203 1071 "The vectors are drawn on a square grid: $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/c0/c053f792a7c1768a15f14990618a54fd75f800a8f0c3d13ebf1524eb37d9574b.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761203 1072 #uuid "6adb066b-ffdb-5044-8b11-d0799ecdd208" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761203 1074 "![Completed vector route](images/03/03ced4f79d7a92dea3de200bb8ea9e59c84dc8b76cfb0e66f1ef8626df167bd7.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $4$ squares left and $4$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares right and $2$ squares down. Thus $OX=2|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-2\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $6$ squares left and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares right and $1$ square down. Thus $XC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=-3\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-2\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\mu=-2$." 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761188 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186761216 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761204 1071 "The vectors are drawn on a square grid: $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/60/60255b61fdbc0e85d64d02c1ef173c7db1706ffc892b2fca2dfb8a2a62f9416d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761204 1072 #uuid "7170985b-076a-5e56-83d9-0fafab8d449c" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761204 1074 "![Completed vector route](images/61/6194a056c4fe39746ab072e5ef1de9a581d266918de114beadf0c9cfd6180212.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $1$ square left and $2$ squares down. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $1$ square right and $2$ squares up. Thus $OX=1|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=-\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $3$ squares right and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square right and $1$ square up. Thus $XC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=3\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=-\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}\\\\&=3\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReorder the terms to match their vector names. Since $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=3$ and $\\mu=-1$." 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761205 1080 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761205 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761205 1083 #uuid "93bfe3a4-9df7-5eef-89f1-f7e86f962fef" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761206 1080 "3" 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761206 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139547582 17592186576411 true] [17592186761206 1083 #uuid "94f930ea-bd58-5fc7-9879-fe42eff10969" 1